BIOMETRIC AND PERSONAL DATA NOTICE

This Software can process biometric data. When the HCEP Desktop bridge is
enabled, the following is received over a local Windows named pipe and used
only to drive in-game character animation:

- real-world gaze vector (pitch, yaw, convergence)
- head pose (pitch, yaw, roll)
- blink state
- a tracked-person identifier
- a cognitive/emotional classification

WHAT IS ACTUALLY IMPLEMENTED

  Transport:      Local Windows named pipe, \\.\pipe\TrueGazeBridge.
                  The pipe is created with an explicit security descriptor
                  restricting access to the creating user (SDDL
                  "D:(A;;GA;;;OW)"). It is not reachable from other user
                  accounts on the machine.
  Storage:        None. Telemetry is held in memory only for the duration of
                  the session and is overwritten continuously. Nothing is
                  written to disk by this Software.
  Retention:      None beyond the session. No history is retained.
  Transmission:   None. No data leaves the local machine.
  Consent:        The bridge is OPT-IN. It is disabled by setting
                  bConnectHcepBridge=false in TrueGaze.ini. Enabling it is the
                  operator's affirmative act.
  Minimisation:   The tracked-person identifier is not required for animation
                  and MAY be zeroed by the HCEP Desktop client without
                  affecting this Software.

WHAT IS NOT IMPLEMENTED

- The payload is NOT encrypted in transit. It is plaintext within the local
    pipe. Security rests on the pipe ACL, not on cryptography.
- There is NO per-connection audit log recording which process connected.
- There is NO automated consent-capture or consent-withdrawal workflow.

OPERATOR RESPONSIBILITY

If you enable the bridge, you are processing the biometric data of whoever is
in front of the camera. Where that person is not you, you are responsible for
obtaining their informed consent. This is especially the case in the European
Union/UK (GDPR Article 9), California (CCPA/CPRA), and Illinois (BIPA), among
other jurisdictions.

This Software does not itself determine whether your use is lawful. It provides
the technical controls listed above; the legal basis for processing is yours.

This notice describes the implementation as of this version and is verified
against the source in GOVERNANCE.md. If the two disagree, that is a defect.

